 # Python to Idiomatic Scala Translation
+
+## Project contract and completion
+
+Before translating a supplied module, establish its source and consumer contract with actual tool output:
+
+1. Read the complete Python source and its public build/test files. For `/root/Tokenizer.py`, inspect `/root/build.sbt` and `/root/TokenizerSpec.scala` first. Derive the package, dependencies and callable signatures from those files.
+2. Account for every source class/function and preserve the consumer-facing API when applying the idioms below. Source-module coverage and existing caller compatibility take priority over redesigning names, constructors or value types.
+3. Compile and run the supplied, unchanged public tests against the exact deliverable in an isolated project. For `/root/Tokenizer.scala`, use:
+
+```bash
+set -euo pipefail &&
+check_dir=$(mktemp -d /tmp/translation-check.XXXXXX) &&
+mkdir -p "$check_dir/src/main/scala" "$check_dir/src/test/scala" "$check_dir/project" &&
+cp /root/build.sbt "$check_dir/build.sbt" &&
+cp /root/localtest/project/build.properties "$check_dir/project/build.properties" &&
+cp /root/Tokenizer.scala "$check_dir/src/main/scala/Tokenizer.scala" &&
+cp /root/TokenizerSpec.scala "$check_dir/src/test/scala/TokenizerSpec.scala" &&
+(cd "$check_dir" && sbt clean test) 2>&1 | tee /root/translation-validation.log
+```
+
+4. Inspect the actual test summary and exit status, repair the deliverable, and repeat the copy-and-test step until the supplied tests execute and all pass. Successful standalone compilation or a self-written smoke test is an intermediate check. Preserve the original build/test files; the isolated project must test the final deliverable rather than a pre-existing implementation. If a dependency blocks validation, report the concrete limitation.
 
